WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.338
Picture scrolling back through your card payments on your phone, newest first, 20 at a time.

2
00:00:08.338 --> 00:00:10.238
The first pages appear at once.

3
00:00:10.238 --> 00:00:13.792
Far back in the list, each new page takes a little longer.

4
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:19.053
And once, a coffee you saw a moment ago turns up again at the top of the next page.

5
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:22.537
So why does page 5,001 cost more than page 1?

6
00:00:22.537 --> 00:00:25.597
And where did that second coffee come from?

7
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:30.904
Behind a list like that, the app asks the database for one page at a time.

8
00:00:30.904 --> 00:00:36.241
The obvious way: sort newest first, skip the pages before this one, and keep 20.

9
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:43.408
In SQL, ORDER BY created_at DESC sorts newest first, and payment_id breaks ties.

10
00:00:43.408 --> 00:00:45.414
LIMIT 20 keeps 20 rows.

11
00:00:45.414 --> 00:00:49.163
And OFFSET 100,000 skips the first 100,000.

12
00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:50.732
That's page 5,001.

13
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:00.436
Let's run it on a table of a million payments, with an index: a separate list of each payment's time and number, kept in order, each pointing to its row.

14
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.009
Page 1 reads the newest 20 entries and stops.

15
00:01:04.009 --> 00:01:06.068
A fraction of a millisecond.

16
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.131
Page 5,001 also returns 20 rows.

17
00:01:09.131 --> 00:01:12.016
So how many entries does it read?

18
00:01:12.016 --> 00:01:13.415
Pause and guess.

19
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:17.258
100,020.

20
00:01:17.258 --> 00:01:25.456
It starts at the newest entry, walks through 100,000 of them, fetches each row, and throws them all away.

21
00:01:25.456 --> 00:01:27.486
Then it keeps the next 20.

22
00:01:27.486 --> 00:01:29.204
About 50 milliseconds.

23
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.559
Page 45,001 reads 900,020 rows, and takes 1.2 seconds.

24
00:01:34.559 --> 00:01:37.330
Same 20 rows out, every time.

25
00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.769
The cost grows with the page number.

26
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.480
Why can't it jump?

27
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.494
Picture the payments as receipts, filed in one long drawer by time.

28
00:01:47.494 --> 00:01:50.412
Someone asks for the 100,001st receipt.

29
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:54.807
No receipt has its position written on it, so you count from the front.

30
00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:55.856
One, two, three...

31
00:01:55.856 --> 00:01:57.897
and you're still counting at lunch.

32
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.581
The index is that drawer.

33
00:02:00.581 --> 00:02:05.354
Each entry holds a time, a payment number, and where its row lives.

34
00:02:05.354 --> 00:02:06.422
Not a position.

35
00:02:06.422 --> 00:02:09.984
So to skip 100,000 entries, PostgreSQL reads them.

36
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.900
Its manual says so: rows skipped by OFFSET still have to be worked out.

37
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.048
The drawer picture breaks here.

38
00:02:17.048 --> 00:02:20.923
A person could at least open the drawer near the middle, by feel.

39
00:02:20.923 --> 00:02:22.473
An index can't even guess.

40
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:24.938
OFFSET has a second problem.

41
00:02:24.938 --> 00:02:29.598
Let's shrink it to five per page, on the course's small table.

42
00:02:29.598 --> 00:02:32.304
Page 1 shows payments 37 down to 33.

43
00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:35.618
While you read it, a new payment arrives: 38.

44
00:02:35.618 --> 00:02:37.880
Everything slides back one place.

45
00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:44.810
Page 2 skips the first five rows, which are now 38 down to 34, so it starts at 33.

46
00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.928
Payment 33, a second time.

47
00:02:46.928 --> 00:02:48.393
That's the coffee.

48
00:02:48.633 --> 00:02:53.581
A deleted row does the opposite: the next page skips a row nobody saw.

49
00:02:53.581 --> 00:02:59.377
A nightly copy, page by page, can copy a payment twice or miss one, with no error.

50
00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:00.933
So here's the wish.

51
00:03:00.933 --> 00:03:05.671
The screen already knows where the last page ended: the last row it showed.

52
00:03:05.671 --> 00:03:09.840
In the drawer, clip a tab behind the last receipt you handed over.

53
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.240
Next time, open the drawer at the tab.

54
00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:18.876
In SQL, the tab is called a cursor: the last row's sort values, its created_at and its payment_id.

55
00:03:18.876 --> 00:03:25.024
The next page asks for the rows after it: WHERE the pair is less than the cursor's two values.

56
00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:33.080
PostgreSQL compares the pairs left to right, the way you'd compare two dates: the year first, and the day only if the years match.

57
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.565
Because the list is newest first, less means further down.

58
00:03:36.833 --> 00:03:38.205
Back to our five.

59
00:03:38.205 --> 00:03:40.141
Page 2 is 32 down to 28.

60
00:03:40.141 --> 00:03:40.948
No repeat.

61
00:03:40.948 --> 00:03:46.353
And the new payment sorts before the tab, so it shows up on page 1.

62
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:54.432
On the million rows, PostgreSQL goes down the index once, straight to the tab, and reads the next 20 entries.

63
00:03:54.432 --> 00:03:57.881
Under a millisecond, on page 2 or on page 5,001.

64
00:03:57.881 --> 00:04:00.252
This is called keyset pagination.

65
00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:01.642
It has a price.

66
00:04:01.642 --> 00:04:08.019
You give up page numbers: a cursor moves one page at a time, so no jumping to page 4,000.

67
00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:12.533
For an endless scroll, or a nightly export, that costs nothing.

68
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.222
Two details decide whether it's right.

69
00:04:15.222 --> 00:04:18.919
The sort must be unique, which is why payment_id is in it.

70
00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:21.405
And the WHERE must compare both values.

71
00:04:21.405 --> 00:04:27.141
Compare created_at alone, and payments at the tab's exact time are skipped, with no error.

72
00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:32.229
So, why was page 5,001 slow, and where did the second coffee come from?

73
00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:36.320
OFFSET counts its way to a page, through every row before it.

74
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.942
That gets slower, and it shifts when rows come and go.

75
00:04:39.942 --> 00:04:42.021
Find the page by a key instead.

76
00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:43.735
One quick check.

77
00:04:43.735 --> 00:04:47.407
Start again: page 1 shows 37 down to 33.

78
00:04:47.407 --> 00:04:50.436
This time, payment 35 is deleted.

79
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.282
With OFFSET 5, which payment does nobody ever see?

80
00:04:54.282 --> 00:04:55.428
Pause and think.

81
00:04:58.633 --> 00:04:58.913
32.

82
00:04:58.913 --> 00:05:03.390
Page 2 skips five rows, and 32 is now the fifth.

83
00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:08.147
With the tab behind 33, page 2 starts at 32 anyway.
